# ploaness Agent Guide

This guide maps the governing standard to the ploaness harness. After it, continue through the lower
`AGENTS.md` layers in the order the standard declares. Exact analyzer rules remain in the executable
harness.

## Layer 1: Governing Standard

- Read `README-guideline-software-project.adoc` before using this ploaness-specific mapping. It states
  the rules themselves; this guide only maps them onto the harness that enforces them.

## Layer 2: Harness Invariants

- The project declares `ploaness` as a dependency and receives its whole contract from it. Do not weaken,
  replace, duplicate, or version the tools, configurations, rules, thresholds, or managed files that
  ploaness owns.
- ploaness governs Payload CMS projects only. It refuses to run when `payload` is not a declared
  dependency.
- Use Node 26 or later, pnpm 11 or later, and a Git working tree.
- Some gates need more than the working tree. `secrets`, `docker`, and `actions` run their analyzer in a
  digest-pinned container and need a reachable Docker daemon; `vulnerabilities` reads the advisory
  database and `deps` reads the package registry. An unreachable daemon, database, or registry fails its
  gate rather than passing it, so read that failure as absent infrastructure rather than as a defect in
  the code.
- Treat every finding and every tool, setup, or compilation failure as a failed verification. Reporting
  findings with `--enforce=false` is not a pass.

## Layer 3: Governed Domains

ploaness governs all of the following domains:

- **Harness wiring:** the scripts, tool-config inheritance, and pinned versions through which the
  project installs ploaness. Five project files carry that inheritance and carry nothing else.
  `eslint.config.mjs`, `vitest.config.mts`, and `playwright.config.ts` are an import of the ploaness
  entry point and its default re-export; one further statement fails the gate. `biome.json` extends
  `ploaness/biome` and declares only its `files` block. `tsconfig.json` extends the literal path
  `ploaness/tsconfig.json`, because Next.js does not read an exports map, and adds only the compiler
  options ploaness leaves open. Those two blocks sit at the member root because Biome and TypeScript
  resolve a relative glob against the directory of the file that declares it, and a glob left inside
  `node_modules` would match nothing in the working tree. A project that rewrites `verify`, appends rules after the harness ESLint
  config, redeclares a Biome section, overrides a compiler option, or replaces the vitest or Playwright
  config fails this domain rather than passing quietly. So does one that redefines a pinned version
  through an install override, or silences the vulnerability gate through `pnpm.auditConfig`. A setting
  may make the harness stricter and never looser. Never name an `@ploaness/...` package: every entry
  point is re-exported through `ploaness` itself, and under the layout pnpm creates, a scoped package the
  project does not depend on will not resolve. Where verification runs is the project's own decision and
  no gate rules on it. `--enforce=false` prints findings and exits zero; a run in that mode is not a
  pass, whoever started it.
- **The two settings no check can verify:** `ploaness.pretest` and `ploaness.testWrapper` are arbitrary
  argv, and a wrapper that swallows its child's exit code is indistinguishable from one that does not
  until it runs. So the rule is stated here rather than enforced. The wrapper exists to put a database
  in front of the suite and nothing else: it passes the child's exit code through unchanged, it does not
  retry, it does not filter output, and it does not decide what runs. A pretest command provisions;
  it does not edit the tree being verified. Either one used to turn a red run green is a bypass, and the
  fact that no gate reports it is the reason it is written down.
- **Repository files:** managed, seeded, and forbidden paths, and an unchanged committable tree during
  verification. Five paths must not exist at all, because ploaness supplies each of those configurations
  itself: `.stylelintrc.json`, `.dependency-cruiser.json`, `knip.json`, `.gitleaks.toml`, and `plugins`.
  The Stylelint configuration ploaness supplies accepts Tailwind v4 as written - every at-rule it
  defines, and the `@import "tailwindcss"` string form it mandates - so a project styled with Tailwind
  needs no configuration of its own. A misspelled at-rule still fails: this is an allowance for a CSS
  dialect, not the rule turned off.
  Writing one to quiet a finding is the reflex this forbids; answer the finding in the source instead.
- **Source:** formatting, fast lint, type-aware lint, strict type checking, type coverage, typography,
  source conventions, committed whitespace and encoding, style sheets, token-bound Tailwind values,
  and module architecture. A public helper carries a documenting comment block, and that block must
  carry a description: an empty `/** */` used to satisfy the rule while documenting nothing. Test
  code is source code and passes the same checks.
- **Payload usage:** bounded Local API reads, no access-control override, a call that passes `user` and
  explicitly sets `overrideAccess: false` so Payload applies that user's permissions, no parent-relative
  imports, and a Local API call reached through `req.payload` that threads `req` so it joins the caller's
  transaction - without it the operation opens its own, cannot see the in-flight write, and is not
  rolled back with it, which is invisible in every passing test until a hook runs inside a transaction -
  and access control decided rather than inherited: every collection decides create, read, update and
  delete, every global decides read and update, and an auth collection caps login attempts and locks
  the account. Payload fills what a config omits during sanitisation, and its default is public read,
  so a partial access block is invisible once the application boots. An upload collection must declare `mimeTypes`: undeclared, it accepts
  whatever a client sends, and an SVG served from this origin is script that runs as the site.
- **Drafts:** a collection or global with `versions.drafts` enabled must not grant a read that admits
  anyone, because `?draft=true` then serves unpublished content to an unauthenticated client. The gate
  catches the inline always-true form; a read delegated to a helper is yours to judge, and the helper
  must require authentication or published status. What the source cannot decide, the access-boundary
  sweep below asks the running application instead.
- **Generated output:** the Payload types and admin import map must match the configuration that
  produces them.
- **Versions:** ploaness owns two sets of them. The toolchain is every package whose version changes
  what a gate does, and it is read from ploaness's own manifests, so there is no second copy to drift.
  The rest is `pins.json`, in four groups, each naming the member kinds that must declare it. `payload`
  (`payload`, `sharp`, `graphql`) is required of a Payload application alone. `web` (`next`, `react`,
  `react-dom`, `@types/react`, `@types/react-dom`) is required of every Next application, whether or
  not Payload is mounted inside it. `runtime-types` (`@types/node`) is required of every member.
  `ecosystem` (`pg`, `@types/pg`, `dotenv`, `cross-env`, `vite`) is matched when declared and required
  of nobody, because a project on MongoDB has no Postgres driver. `vite` sits there because ploaness's
  own test runner and React plugin resolve a vite of their own, and that plugin declares vite as a
  PEER, so a project declaring a different one gets a loading failure rather than mere duplication.
  A type package is pinned for the toolchain's reason: it is an input
  to tsc, so a patch release changes what type-checks while the project stays unchanged. `packageManager`
  and `engines` are pinned too - Corepack runs exactly the package manager named, so it decides how
  every other pin resolves. `engines.pnpm` is the exact version `packageManager` names rather than a
  floor, for the reason no dependency carries a range; and `preflight` reads the pnpm actually running
  the command, because a declaration Corepack is not enabled to obey resolves the tree with something
  else. Declare each at the exact version ploaness pins, and every
  `@payloadcms/*` package at the pinned `payload` version, because Payload fails at runtime when its
  own packages disagree. No dependency carries a range: a caret, tilde, comparator, wildcard or
  partial version means the build and the suite run against whatever was newest at install time. An
  install override may redefine a package the project does NOT declare, which is the only way to
  reach a transitive advisory. It may not redefine one the project declares, nor one ploaness pins even
  where the project declares neither, and `overrides`, `resolutions`, `patchedDependencies`, and
  `packageExtensions` in `package.json` are read exactly as the workspace file is.
- **Dependencies:** licenses, known vulnerabilities, available stable updates, the maximum permitted
  freshness gap, dead code, unused dependencies, and the allowlist naming which dependencies may run a
  build or postinstall script. An empty allowlist is a valid answer; an undeclared one is not, because
  it lets every package in the resolved set execute code at install time. A vulnerability is resolved by
  upgrading the package. Record an exception only when no upgrade exists, and an exception that
  suppresses nothing fails.
- **Accessibility:** a sweep ploaness pins into `tests/e2e/a11y.e2e.spec.ts` crawls the public routes
  reachable from the home page and runs axe on each. A route's default state is scanned in full; the
  `:hover` and `:focus` states of its interactive controls are scanned for contrast alone, which is the
  defect those states actually hide. Site chrome is swept once, because one stylesheet renders it
  everywhere, and the content of `main` is swept per route. The crawl stops and fails at 250 routes, so a
  passing sweep never describes a site whose remaining pages were silently left unread. It is a managed file: edit it and the
  assets gate reports drift. What the project declares about it is the origin (`ploaness.serverUrl`),
  any further route prefix the crawl must not follow (`ploaness.accessibilitySkipRoutes`, which adds to
  `/admin` and `/api` rather than replacing them), and a smaller route ceiling than the shipped one
  (`ploaness.accessibilityRouteBudget`, honoured only downward). Reaching the ceiling is a failure
  rather than a truncation, and the answer to it is the harness raising the ceiling after measuring:
  narrowing the crawl to fit means excusing real public pages from accessibility checking so that the
  accessibility check passes. A finding is a defect in the page, never in the sweep:
  repair the markup or the contrast rather than narrowing what is scanned.
  The sweep skips `/admin` and `/api` and always will: the panel is Payload's markup, not the
  project's, and the crawl carries no credential to sign in with. A CUSTOM admin view is the one thing
  inside that exemption the project wrote itself, so the `admin-views` gate requires the project to
  have scanned it: a configuration declaring a view under `admin.components.views` must be matched by a
  spec under `tests/` that drives that route and reaches axe, directly or through a helper it imports.
  ploaness cannot run that scan - it cannot sign in, and it does not know which container belongs to
  the project rather than to the framework - so scope the scan to the view's own container, because a
  scan of the whole panel reports defects in components nobody in the project can repair.
- **Security headers:** a sweep ploaness pins into `tests/e2e/security-headers.e2e.spec.ts` asks the
  running application what it answers with. A Next.js application sends no security header unless the
  project configures one, and no static check can see that it did not: the page renders and the build
  passes. The baseline is `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, a `Referrer-Policy`, a decision about who
  may frame the page, and a `Content-Security-Policy` that constrains script without readmitting
  `'unsafe-inline'`. In practice that means a per-request nonce from a `middleware.ts`, because the
  suite drives `next dev` and its inline scripts would otherwise need blanket permission.
- **Access boundary:** a sweep ploaness pins into `tests/e2e/access-boundary.e2e.spec.ts` asks Payload
  itself, through `/api/access` with no credential, what an unauthenticated caller may do to every
  collection and global. The static rules see that each operation was decided and can see an inline
  always-true rule; they cannot see what `read: anyone` decides, nor what Payload made of the config
  after sanitisation, so a project can satisfy all of them and still serve every document to every
  stranger. The admin panel must refuse an unauthenticated caller, and every permission the application
  grants anonymously must be one the project recorded under `ploaness.publicAccess` with the reason it
  is intended. Close the access rule, or record the grant; a grant nobody wrote is the finding.
- **Test determinism:** two rules of the governing standard that used to be prose and are now checks.
  The suite runs under a network guard the shipped setup file installs before any setup of yours: a
  socket, a DNS lookup, or a fetch that leaves this machine fails the test that made it, naming the
  destination and quoting the rule. Loopback passes, so does an address this machine itself carries, and
  so does a unix socket - a database on `127.0.0.1` is the intended case. Every resolver query fails,
  loopback included, because a resolver sends a packet to a nameserver rather than reading a hosts file,
  which is why a `mongodb+srv://` connection string cannot be used from a test. A service that exists
  only outside the machine is exercised through a local component that implements its protocol; there is
  no setting that widens the guard, because widening it is the thing the rule forbids. Raw datagrams,
  child or cluster processes, and workers are rejected by the lint pass because each creates a transport
  or runtime outside the installed guard. The suite also
  runs shuffled - both the files and the tests within them - under a seed ploaness owns, so a test that
  only passed in the order its file happened to be written in now fails. Repair the coupling: make each
  test establish what it needs. `it.concurrent`, `it.sequential`, and `vi.setConfig` are rejected,
  because each one buys a green run by reintroducing the coupling the shuffle exists to find.
- **Suppressions:** the suppression count is capped against the size of the source: two, plus one for
  every 500 non-blank lines of the project's own code. Every run reports the count and the distance left.
  When the ceiling is reached, the way to spend a suppression is to remove another one - not to raise the
  ceiling, which no setting can do. A suppression inside a managed file is ploaness's and is not counted,
  so it is also not yours to remove.
- **Assets and documentation:** image integrity, documentation references, config references, and
  the agent skill manifest. `AGENTS.md` holds the rules; the entry-point file any other coding agent
  reads - `GEMINI.md`, `.cursorrules`, `.windsurfrules`, `.clinerules`,
  `.github/copilot-instructions.md` - is optional, and one that exists carries nothing but a pointer at
  `AGENTS.md`. Write the pointer in whatever form that tool understands. Anything else in the file is a
  second contract, and two agents answering to two contracts is the thing this refuses.
- **Repository assurance:** secret scanning, container and workflow linting, test execution with
  per-file coverage, commit-message policy, commit typography, and linear history.

Do not treat a domain omitted from one command's output as ungoverned. The shipped configurations and
the ploaness gates together define the executable contract.

### Workspaces

ploaness governs a repository rather than a directory. A repository holding one package is the case
where those are the same thing, and it is what most projects are: nothing here changes anything for it.

A **member** is a package ploaness governs, and a package is a member when it declares `ploaness` as a
dependency. That is not a switch to flip. Under pnpm's strict layout a package that does not depend on
the harness cannot resolve `ploaness/biome` or `ploaness/eslint` from its own configuration files, so
its wiring could not work whatever it declared. A pnpm project that is neither a member nor inside a
member's `sourceRoots` is reported by the `wiring` gate, so removing the declaration to escape the gates
is loud rather than silent.

Each member has a **kind**, derived from what it declares and never from what it says about itself. A
package cannot receive a weaker configuration by describing itself differently.

| Kind | Derived from | What it receives |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Payload application | declares `payload` | Every gate, every managed file, and the Payload-shaped configurations. |
| application | declares `next`, not `payload` | Everything except the Payload rules and the access-boundary sweep. A Next.js frontend reading a CMS over HTTP still has a build, a client bundle, and a browser suite to govern. |
| library | declares neither | The framework-neutral configurations - `ploaness/biome-core`, `ploaness/tsconfig-core.json`, `ploaness/eslint-library`, `ploaness/vitest-library` - and no browser configuration, because it has no application to drive. |

Every gate judges one of three **scopes**: `repository` for the tracked tree, the dependency graph, the
history, and the managed files a tool reads from the tree root; `package` for one member's own source,
configuration, and suite; `payload` for the rules about Payload itself. The install-script allowlist and
the `overrides` block are read at repository scope and nowhere else, because that is the only place pnpm
honours them.

The run order is the registry's order, unchanged. A repository with one member produces exactly the
sequence ploaness ran before members existed, and a repository with several interleaves them, with the
run log naming the member each gate was asked about.

A member declares its own settings under the `ploaness` key of its own `package.json`, layered onto
whatever the repository root declared. A list a project adds to stays additive, a threshold is honoured
only when it is stricter, and a command list or an origin describes one package, so the member's answer
stands. `ploaness init` writes each member the project files its own kind is judged against, and the
three project scripts once, at the repository root.

### Project Layout

Module architecture is a shape rather than a preference, and four rules of it follow from neither the
governing standard nor Payload's own documentation.

- `src/access` and `src/lib` are the pure-logic floor: they import nothing else under `src/`, so the code
  deciding who may read a document stays testable without booting the framework. Nothing outside
  `src/app` imports `src/app`, which is the top layer and composes the rest.
- A collection, global, field, or block config is declarative wiring, so no function is written inline in
  one. Access rules, hooks, and validators live in `src/access` or `src/lib` and are imported by
  reference, because a function inlined into a config carries no seam a unit test can reach.
- **A new environment variable lands in every place that needs it, in one commit**: the validated
  module that reads it, the example file that documents it for a clone, and - when a compose file
  interpolates it - the `env:` block of every workflow that verifies. Nothing links those places, so
  the omission surfaces somewhere other than where it was made: a clone booting on a default it never
  chose, or `docker compose config` failing on CI alone. The `environment` gate holds them together.
- `process.env` is read only in `src/lib/environment.ts`, `payload.config.ts` included. That module
  validates and narrows what it reads and every other module consumes the typed values it returns, so
  the environment surface stays auditable in one place.
- A spec is collected only from `tests/unit/**/*.unit.spec.ts`, `tests/int/**/*.int.spec.ts`, or
  `tests/e2e`. A file outside those paths never runs, and the per-file coverage floor then fails on the
  code that file was written to cover, which reads as a coverage defect rather than as a misplaced test.

### Commit Messages

Exact analyzer rules stay in the executable harness. This domain is the one exception. No format command
repairs a commit message, no source file holds it, and a published one cannot be rewritten. An agent
satisfies the policy only while writing the message, so the policy is declared here in full.

| Rule | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Header | `type(scope): subject` |
| Type | one of `build`, `chore`, `ci`, `docs`, `feat`, `fix`, `perf`, `refactor`, `test` |
| Scope | optional, and when present drawn from `a-z`, `0-9`, and `-` |
| Subject | 15 characters or more, with the whole header at most 72 characters |
| Subject ending | no trailing period |
| Junk words | `wip`, `tmp`, `temp`, `misc`, `stuff`, `asdf`, `fixup`, anywhere in the subject |
| Body | every line after the header, required past 2 changed files or 50 added plus deleted lines |
| Typography | plain ASCII only: no em dash, en dash, one-character ellipsis, or curly quotation mark |
| Attribution | no marker naming an AI agent or an agent session, in any commit |

There are no exemptions. Every commit is held to every rule, including the ones git writes for you: a
merge, a revert, or an autosquash commit must be given a conforming subject by hand. There is no
`revert` type - a revert is described by what it does, like any other change.

Three traps account for most failures:

- The 15-character floor rejects a short subject such as `fix typo`.
- The body threshold counts added and deleted lines together, so a routine change crosses it quickly.
- A junk word is rejected anywhere in the subject, not only as its first word, so `fix: clear the tmp
  directory` fails. Name the thing in full.

Check a commit as soon as it is recorded, with `ploaness commit-message --range HEAD~1..HEAD`. The
command reads the repository rather than the build, so an unpublished commit can still be amended.

### History Shape

The history is linear, so a merge commit is prohibited wherever it sits, including between two side
branches. A branch takes the work of another by `git rebase` or `git cherry-pick`. The check counts the
parents Git recorded rather than reading the header, so a subject that mentions merging is an ordinary
commit, and rewording a merge does not hide it.

The rule reaches back to the first commit and has no exception. Avoid a merge rather than plan to repair
one: once it is published, only a fresh history satisfies the rule.

## Layer 4: Command Workflow

`ploaness init` writes three project scripts, so `pnpm run verify`, `pnpm run verify:full`, and
`pnpm run format` invoke the commands below.

1. Run `ploaness sync` only to create or restore managed repository files, then review and commit its
   edits.
2. Run `ploaness format` (`pnpm run format`) to apply formatting, then review the resulting source.
3. Run `ploaness verify` (`pnpm run verify`) for Default verification.
4. Run `ploaness verify --extended` (`pnpm run verify:full`) before finishing. Extended verification
   includes Default verification and adds history, build, bundle, and end-to-end checks.

A run stops at the first failing gate and names how many gates it did not reach, so it reports the
findings of one gate rather than of all of them. Repair that gate and rerun. Nothing below a failing
gate has been verified: a gate is free to leave the tree in a shape the next one misreads, and a column
of passes beneath a failure describes a state nobody established. A passing freshness gate still prints
its update report, because an available version below the failure bound is information rather than a
third verdict.

`--enforce=false` is the exception, and the reason the flag exists. A report-only run carries on past a
failing gate, so one run lists every finding a project has instead of handing them over one rerun at a
time. Reach for it to survey what is outstanding, not to finish. It still exits 0 and it is still not
a pass. `preflight` and `wiring` stop it anyway: below a failing one, ploaness either may not judge this
project or cannot tell whether what it is judging is what it thinks.

Run a single gate with `ploaness gate <id>`, and `ploaness gates` to list every gate in run order with
the tier it belongs to. Use it freely while iterating and to answer one focused question - `ploaness
gate deps` to see what has a newer release, `ploaness gate suppressions` to see where the budget
stands. What a single gate is not is a verdict: it skips `preflight`, so it never establishes that this
project is one ploaness may judge, and it says nothing about the gates it did not run. Only a full run
answers "is this ready".

A gate normally reports a verdict rather than a transcript, and `ploaness gate <id> --verbose` prints
what its underlying tool actually wrote - passing or failing, whole and unfiltered. Reach for it to
audit a verdict rather than take it on faith: "no vulnerability at or above moderate" is a claim, and
the scanner's own output is the evidence for it. A full run has no such flag, because thirty-eight
transcripts bury the one finding the report exists to surface.

### Default Verification Gates

Default verification runs 34 gates, in the order below. A repository-scope gate runs once; a
package-scope gate runs once per member; a Payload-scope gate runs only for the members that declare
Payload. `tree-verify` is last in both tiers, because the extended gates are the ones most able to
rewrite a tracked file.

| Gate | Scope | What it verifies |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `preflight` | repository | The repository meets the harness requirements and holds a Payload member. |
| `wiring` | repository | The scripts, the five project files, and the pinned versions are intact and nothing undoes them. |
| `assets` | repository | Every managed file matches what ploaness ships. |
| `tree-snapshot` | repository | Records a fingerprint of the working tree. |
| `types` | package | `tsc --noEmit`. |
| `biome` | package | Formatting and the fast lint pass. |
| `biome-schema` | repository | The `$schema` in `biome.json` names the installed Biome version. |
| `eslint` | package | The type-aware lint pass. |
| `conventions` | repository | The typography ban and the hand-written JavaScript ban, over every tracked text file, and documenting comments left above another comment rather than above what they document. |
| `editorconfig` | repository | Every tracked text file matches the pinned `.editorconfig`, and no code line passes 120 characters. |
| `suppressions` | package | The suppression count against the earned ceiling, over the project's own files. |
| `css` | package | Stylelint over every style sheet under the source roots, with Tailwind v4 accepted as written. |
| `tailwind-tokens` | package | Every visual value in the markup comes from the theme rather than an arbitrary value. |
| `arch` | package | dependency-cruiser: layering, cycles, and orphans. |
| `type-coverage` | package | Type coverage of 100% over hand-written TypeScript. |
| `payload-generated` | payload | The generated Payload types and admin import map are current. |
| `generated-denial` | repository | The agent runtime is denied write access to every generated artefact. |
| `payload-rules` | package | The Payload usage rules, including access control and drafts. |
| `admin-views` | payload | Every custom admin view is scanned for accessibility by a spec of the project's own. |
| `config-refs` | package | No analyzer setting carves out a path that no longer exists or matches nothing. |
| `secrets` | repository | gitleaks over the git history, plus the declared `secretAllowlist`. |
| `licenses` | repository | The license of every dependency sits inside the allowlist. |
| `vulnerabilities` | repository | No resolved package carries an advisory at or above the declared severity. |
| `install-scripts` | repository | The allowlist naming which dependencies may run a build or postinstall script. |
| `deps` | repository | No declared dependency is two or more major versions behind. Pinned images are reported, never failed. |
| `image-assets` | repository | Every tracked image decodes as the format its bytes claim. |
| `environment` | repository | Every variable the application reads, and every one a compose file interpolates, is documented in the example file and supplied by each verifying workflow. |
| `docs` | repository | The agent documentation names no script and no file that has gone. |
| `skills` | repository | The metadata header of every `SKILL.md`. |
| `docker` | repository | hadolint over every Dockerfile, and `docker compose config`. |
| `actions` | repository | actionlint over every workflow. |
| `knip` | package | Dead code, unused files, and unused dependencies. |
| `tests` | package | The suite and its per-file coverage floors, under the network guard and the shuffle. |
| `tree-verify` | repository | The working tree is unchanged since `tree-snapshot` ran. |

### Extended Verification Gates

Extended verification adds six gates to the tier above.

| Gate | Scope | What it verifies |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `require-full-history` | repository | The clone carries the whole history. |
| `commit-history` | repository | Every commit message in the history. |
| `linear-history` | repository | No merge commit anywhere in the history. |
| `build` | package | The production build. |
| `bundle` | package | The gzipped client JavaScript stays under the bundle budget. |
| `e2e` | package | The end-to-end suite, including the three sweeps ploaness ships into it. |

## Layer 5: Exceptions and Repairs

- Fix a managed-file finding with `ploaness sync`. A project may take over a managed path through
  `ploaness.unmanagedAssets` only when the assignment explicitly requires it, and the entry must record
  the reason. An entry without a reason is ignored.
- `AGENTS.md` is the one managed file the project also writes in. ploaness owns the block between the
  `BEGIN PLOANESS MANAGED INSTRUCTIONS` and `END PLOANESS MANAGED INSTRUCTIONS` markers; it leads the
  file and appears exactly once, and the project's own instructions go below it, where `sync` carries
  them through untouched. Duplicated or incomplete markers are the one case `sync` refuses, because
  every edit it could make there would either duplicate the block or swallow text the project owns.
  Repair those by hand.
- Fix formatting findings with `ploaness format`. Fix type, lint, architecture, Payload, dependency,
  test, coverage, history, and security findings at their source. Do not disable the failing check.
- An exclusion narrows a gate, which ploaness permits only by file role. Each entry is a `pattern` and
  the `reason` it is excluded, and the reason states the role: what that file IS, not that testing or
  checking it is inconvenient. An entry without a reason fails the `wiring` gate and is dropped as well,
  so the narrowing it asked for never takes effect. An entry that matches nothing the gate would have
  judged is a finding too: it leaves the report reading exactly as it would have read without it, and it
  outlives the file it was written for.
- There is no setting that turns a gate off. If a gate is wrong for the project, that is a ploaness bug
  to be fixed in ploaness, not a local override.
- For a source suppression, use the analyzer's exact rule identifier and put the reason on the same
  line. A bare suppression without a stated reason is itself a finding, and every suppression counts
  against the ceiling.

### Change Requests

A project cannot repair the harness from inside itself, and that is the design rather than a gap in it:
a rule a project can edit is not a rule. What follows from it is that a rule improves only when somebody
reports it, which makes the report part of the contract rather than a courtesy.

Raise it with the ploaness maintainers when:

- a gate blocks work that is correct, and the only way past it is a suppression or a carve-out;
- the same finding is repaired the same way in project after project, which is a rule that belongs in
  the harness rather than in each project's habits;
- a project-owned check turns out to be true of every project of this kind, and should move into a gate;
- a message names a defect without naming the repair, or names the wrong repair.

Bring the failing output, the smallest case that reproduces it, and what the rule should have said. A
suppression to get past a gate is a local answer to a general problem: take it when the work cannot
wait, and report it either way, because the next project meets the same gate with no memory of this one.

### Settings

Every setting lives under the `ploaness` key of `package.json`, and every one is optional. A list-valued
setting adds to the shipped default rather than replacing it, and a malformed value falls back to that
default, so a typo can never widen a rule.

| Setting | Default | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `sourceRoots` | `src`, `tests`, `scripts` | Where the project's own source lives. Added to, never narrowed. |
| `unmanagedAssets` | empty | Managed paths the project takes over. `path` and `reason`. |
| `typographyExclusions` | the generated Payload files | Paths the typography ban skips, as a regex. |
| `javascriptAllowlist` | `eslint.config.mjs`, `importMap.js` | Paths the hand-written JavaScript ban skips, as a regex. |
| `coverageExclude` | the framework glue, the generated files, the components | Paths left out of coverage, as a glob. |
| `frameworkGlue` | `src/app/(payload)/**`, `src/payload.config.ts` | Paths holding framework-written scaffolding, which the lint pass relaxes rather than judges. Added to. |
| `generatedArtefacts` | where Payload puts them in a stock installation | Paths a generator owns, which no rule judges and no agent may edit. Added to, for an admin panel mounted elsewhere. |
| `pureLogicRoots` | `src/access`, `src/lib` | Directories that may depend on nothing else under the source root. Added to. |
| `bundleBudgetBytes` | `1126400` | The ceiling on gzipped client JavaScript. Only a smaller value is honoured. |
| `maxSuppressions` | the earned ceiling | A stricter ceiling; `0` permits none. A looser value is ignored. |
| `vulnerabilitySeverity` | `moderate` | The severity that fails the gate. Only a stricter value is honoured. |
| `vulnerabilityAllowlist` | empty | `advisory`, `reason`, and `addedOn`. An entry missing one is dropped. An advisory carried by ploaness's own dependencies is recorded by ploaness, not here. |
| `secretAllowlist` | empty | Fake credentials committed as fixtures. `path` and `reason`. |
| `serverUrl` | `http://localhost:3000` | The origin the end-to-end suite drives. |
| `auxiliaryServers` | empty | Servers the end-to-end run starts beside the application. `command` and `url`. An entry missing either is dropped. The application is still started first and still answers at `serverUrl`. |
| `publicAccess` | empty | Anonymous permissions the project grants on purpose. `entity`, `operation`, and `reason`. An entry missing one is dropped, which restores the finding rather than excusing it. |
| `accessibilitySkipRoutes` | `/admin`, `/api` | Route prefixes the sweep skips, added to those two. A route is a URL, so the reach rule above does not judge it. |
| `accessibilityRouteBudget` | `250` | How many routes the sweep may discover before it stops and fails. Only a smaller value is honoured. |
| `analysisEnv` | `PAYLOAD_SECRET`, `DATABASE_URL` | Placeholder environment for the gates that import the project. |
| `pretest` | empty | A command run before the `tests`, `build`, and `e2e` gates. |
| `testWrapper` | empty | An argument list wrapping the process those gates run. |

Two of these answer a finding that has no other answer. A fake credential committed as a test fixture is
declared through `secretAllowlist`, because `.gitleaks.toml` is forbidden and ploaness renders the
scanner's configuration outside the working tree with the scanner's own rules still on, so an entry can
only add a named exception and never replaces anything. And a Payload configuration reads `process.env`
as the module loads, which a gate that only imports the project cannot satisfy in a bare shell;
`analysisEnv` supplies placeholders for exactly those gates, while the gates that run the application
use the real environment. That environment reaches the end-to-end run through the Playwright
configuration, which reads `.env.local` and then `.env` into its own process before a spec is
collected, because a helper seeding through `getPayload` loads the Payload config at import time and
the bare re-export leaves the project nowhere to read them itself. Neither file replaces a variable
that is already set, so a value exported by CI or a service container still outranks both. The
`tests` gate reads the same two files, from the setup file ploaness loads first, so an integration
spec that boots the project finds its database without the project arranging it. A project that
still loads them in its own `vitest.setup.ts` is unaffected.

## Layer 6: Rules No Check Verifies

A rule that no automated check can verify reliably binds with the same force as one that a gate
enforces. These are decided by reading the code against the wording below, not by a passing run.

- A test fails when the behavior its name states is removed from the code under test. A test that keeps
  passing after its subject is deleted is measuring nothing, however green the suite is.
- A test verifies one behavior and states that behavior in its name.
- An assertion whose verdict does not depend on the code under test is banned. The gate catches the
  literal form, such as an assertion that a constant is true. It cannot catch the same mistake made
  through a variable, and that is still the same mistake.
- A component's name decides nothing about whether it is real. An in-memory library component that
  implements a protocol is real; a class inside the test tree whose answers a test sets is a
  substitute, whatever it is called. The bans on the known mocking libraries cover the named cases, not
  a hand-rolled fake.
- Every wait a test performs is bounded by a timeout sized so that its expiry signals a defect of the
  code rather than the speed of the machine. The reach beyond this machine is a check now; how long a
  test is willing to wait is still a reading.
- A test does not shell out, start a worker, or open a raw datagram. Each leaves the guarded runtime, so
  the lint pass rejects the API before the suite runs.
- A callable does not return null, and does not accept null, where an optional or result type says the
  same thing. A callable that cannot do its job fails immediately with an error that says what went
  wrong, rather than falling back silently to a default.
- One concept has one name everywhere in the project, and a name consists of full words. An
  abbreviation appears only where it is more recognizable than the full form.
- A dependency cycle is broken by moving the shared concept into a unit both sides depend on, or by
  inverting one direction behind an abstraction. A cycle is never declared acceptable.
- The attribution ban binds in prose as well as by pattern: a marker naming an AI agent that no pattern
  yet knows is still a violation.
- A URL that came from a CMS field is passed through `safeHref` before it reaches an `href`. React
  escapes text content, so an editor cannot inject markup through a field, but an href is an attribute:
  `javascript:alert(1)` stored in a link field executes as the site the moment a visitor clicks it, and
  nothing in the framework, the type system, or the lint pass reports it. The helper is shipped so no
  project has to get the scheme rules right twice; calling it where editor content becomes a link is
  the part no check can see.
  - Import it from `@ploaness/runtime`. Nothing has to be installed first: the `wiring` gate requires
    every application to declare that package in `dependencies`, so it is already there. It is a
    separate package precisely so this import is legal - `ploaness` is a devDependency, and the `arch`
    gate forbids `src/**` from importing one because a devDependency is absent from a production
    install. Do not resolve that by moving `ploaness` into `dependencies`, which ships ESLint,
    Playwright and the rest of the analyzer toolchain to production, and do not carve an exception
    into `not-to-dev-dep`, which is reporting a real hazard. `@ploaness/runtime` is pure and declares
    no dependencies of its own, which is what makes it safe to ship.
  - From `tests/**`, `ploaness/runtime` re-exports the same function, and either import is fine.
